{
  "program": {
    "name": "Steady Strength",
    "goal": "Gentle holds and light strength work to keep the core, legs, wrists and shoulders strong and stable",
    "lengthWeeks": 8,
    "restDay": 7,
    "sessionOrder": ["warmup", "balance", "isometric", "strength", "core", "cooldown", "walk"],
    "rules": [
      "Keep the spine neutral and tall. No sit-ups, crunches, toe touches, twisting under load or bending forward to lift, and move slowly and in control.",
      "No jumping, running or heavy weights. Use a resistance band, body weight or something light (a 0.5–1 kg tin or bottle, or a 1–2 kg bag for the suitcase hold).",
      "Effort is 5 to 6 out of 10: finish every set with 2 or 3 reps still in hand. Mild muscle tiredness is normal; pain is the signal to stop.",
      "Breathe out during the effort and never hold your breath. Holds stay short, at about half effort.",
      "Almost everything is standing at a counter, sitting on a chair or leaning on a wall, with the supported version first and less support as you progress. The one lying-down hold (the glute bridge) can be done on a bed and has a standing alternative."
    ],
    "safety": [
      "This is not medical advice. Check with your doctor or physiotherapist before starting, and show them this plan, especially with a history of slipped discs in the lower back, osteoporosis, heart or blood pressure problems, or a recent fall or operation.",
      "Stop and get advice if you feel sharp pain, or pain, numbness, tingling or weakness that spreads into a leg or arm.",
      "Get urgent medical help if you notice numbness around the groin or buttocks, or new problems controlling your bladder or bowels.",
      "Breathe out during the effort and never hold your breath. Holds are short on purpose.",
      "Keep a kitchen counter, a sturdy chair or a wall within reach for every standing exercise."
    ]
  },
